# Sizing the Local Desk: A Practical RAM × Model Matrix for 16 GB Mac Minis Running Ollama

## The Memory Budget Reality

Do not assume "16 GB" means 16 GB of usable memory for inference. macOS reserves a significant baseline for system services, kernel buffers, and background daemons, which typically consumes between 2 GB and 3 GB on Apple silicon. Furthermore, your local stack includes Hugo, the static site generator, and Freqtrade running in dry_run mode. While Freqtrade is lightweight, it still occupies process space; Hugo, depending on the theme complexity, can easily consume another 500 MB to 1 GB. Consequently, the actual headroom available for the Large Language Model (LLM) often shrinks to roughly 11 GB to 12 GB of contiguous, addressable RAM. Any model exceeding this threshold risks triggering a hard kill from the system or causing severe swapping, which destroys inference speed and stability.

## The Qwen Selection Strategy

The selection of a quantised model is not merely about parameter count but the specific footprint of the GGUF file and its runtime memory requirements. For a 16 GB architecture, the Qwen 4B family generally offers the most safety margin, often fitting comfortably within the 11–12 GB limit even with a context window expanded to 32k or 128k tokens. The Qwen 9B variant sits in a precarious middle ground; while it may load initially, it frequently pushes memory pressure high enough to cause stuttering or swap usage when running other applications simultaneously. The newer Qwen 3.5 variants introduce efficiency gains through architectural updates, but their base size often rivals the older 9B models in terms of raw VRAM/RAM consumption, requiring careful scrutiny of the specific GGUF variant chosen.

